WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.740
- The real secret of everything

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.051
comes from this yellow pad.

00:00:03.051 --> 00:00:03.884
(crowd oohing)

00:00:03.884 --> 00:00:06.590
Hey, guys, welcome to
my night-time routine.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.620
I'm gonna be sharing with you

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.560
everything that I do before I go to sleep,

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.150
as well as the scientific
reasoning for why I do it.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.040
I'm trying to get the
best night sleep possible.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.020
I wanna drive down my anxiety levels,

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.720
especially during the stressful time.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.370
And finally three, I'm wanna get myself

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.130
ready to face tomorrow as
the best version of myself.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.410
Before we get started,
let's give a huge shout out

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.350
to Sensodyne for sponsoring this video.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:27.320
We're starting in the bedroom.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.123
The bedroom, bedroom, bedroom, bedroom.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.470
The reason why I'm starting here,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.470
even though it's not bedtime just yet,

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.730
is because I wanna prep
my sleep environment

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.830
to be ready by the time I get to bed.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.600
I really focus on three things here.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.590
Temperature, humidity, and light levels.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.140
These all need to be set ahead of time,

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.550
preferably two, three hours,

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:47.920
so by the time you get to bed

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.760
you're not rolling around
trying to fall asleep.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.170
That's gonna peak your anxiety

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.170
and anxiety is the enemy of good sleep.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.290
The first thing I do is
I set the temperature.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.820
I ideally look for 65 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.720
The reason I do this is 'cause your body

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.630
naturally lowers its core
temperature while you sleep.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.410
So if you can assist in the process

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.240
by lowering the environment temperature,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.710
you're gonna be doing yourself a favor.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.550
If you don't, and it's warm in your room,

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.680
your body's gonna try to
cool down by sweating.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.410
And have you ever slept in sweaty sheets?

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.450
That's a bad night sleep.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.540
Next, I flip on my humidifier.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.600
The air can get quite dry in my apartment

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.140
because I am running the AC.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.210
And dry air really irritates
the nasal passageways.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.170
Leading to congestion,
scratchy throat, chapped lips.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.250
It's really difficult to get
a good night sleep that way.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.020
And I actually recommend a humidifier

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.280
to many of my patients in order to keep

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.360
their mucous membrane moist to potentially

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.430
decrease the likelihood
of them getting a virus.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.890
The last piece of the puzzle

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.510
is to get rid of all
external sources of light,

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.020
and to make that happen I have
to close my blackout shades.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.130
Scientific research has actually shown

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.130
that even small sources of light

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.340
can make it difficult for you

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.400
to fall asleep and to stay asleep.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.370
I have made it complete
blackout situation in my room

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.740
and I really noticed the difference

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:55.820
over the last year or two.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:56.940
We've wrapped up in the bedroom,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.210
let's take it to bathroom.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.510
First things first, getting
rid of the facial hair.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:01.890
Now I used to shave before,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.350
but then I realized it made me look

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.770
way too young to be a doctor.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.767
I kept getting those
comments from my patients,

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.210
"You're Doogie Howser."

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.043
It was too much.

00:02:09.043 --> 00:02:10.480
So now I use a beard trimmer

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.240
and really get that hair nice and short.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.570
As well as using a shaver
in order to get rid

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:15.750
of scruff around my neck.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.220
I think it still looks professional

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.350
but at the same time, it's
a pretty aesthetic look.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.520
Next, I wanna give you a sneak
peek at my skin care routine.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.370
Generally, I like to keep a pretty simple

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.070
with a Murad cleanser
followed by a moisturizer.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.320
But on other days, I like
to get a little fancy

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.730
and I'm gonna show you that whole process

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.500
when I do a dedicated skin care video.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.470
Keep a look out for that.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.470
Last, but certainly not least,

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.360
is taking care of these pearly whites.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.610
Look, your oral health
is intimately connected

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.080
to the health of the rest of your body.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.210
We've actually seen
with scientific research

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.760
that those who have poor oral health

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.960
have poor cardiovascular outcomes as well.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.770
You've gotta brush your
teeth at least twice a day.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.110
Something you may not know about me

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:51.990
is that I have really sensitive teeth.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.560
I've had braces twice in my life,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.610
whitened my teeth probably once too much,

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.210
and because of that it's quite painful

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.980
to drink cold liquids like those

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.170
cookies and cream milkshakes.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.850
Something I've been doing to combat it

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.500
is using Sensodyne
sensitivity and gum toothpaste

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.670
because it truly helps.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.910
This is targeted for people

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.230
who have sensitive teeth of gum problems.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.110
It's actually the number one
dentist recommended brand

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.340
for sensitive teeth,
definitely check it out.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.033
Can't forget to feed the newf.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:21.600
Now the newf's gotta poop.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.100
Walking the newf during quarantine.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.380
Sound might not be great.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:27.620
This is actually my favorite part

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.650
of my night-time routine

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.580
because it really helps me wind down.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.270
It's dark, it's quiet.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:35.490
Nobody's out, it's a ghost
town except the newf!

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.440
The bear finally pooped.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.120
Now it's time for me to go upstairs

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.540
and share with you a couple secrets

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.840
that I do to lower my anxiety before bed.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.220
This is when my wind-down truly begins.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.770
And I'm gonna take you
through the few steps

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:49.030
that I have laid out here

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.830
of things you need to do
in order to get into bed

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.190
and fall asleep really quickly.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.203
First things first, I know the lights

00:03:54.203 --> 00:03:55.990
in here aren't that dim

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:57.460
because I have the lights
I'm recording with,

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.270
but generally I only have that light

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.070
and that light on to keep it dim,

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.730
so that when I get to
bed, my melatonin levels

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.040
are at their highest point.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.070
Melatonin is a sleepiness hormone

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:08.540
that gets released when it's dark.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.180
It's part of your
natural circadian rhythm.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.190
So the last thing you wanna do

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.200
is turn on all the lights
right before you go to sleep

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.680
and hurt that melatonin release.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.140
You wanna keep that as high as possible.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.947
Second, I have my sleepy time tea here.

00:04:18.947 --> 00:04:19.780
(slurping)

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.760
I love green tea, but
green tea has caffeine.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.220
You don't wanna consume
caffeine before bed.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:25.510
Even if you're able to fall asleep,

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:27.580
it's not a good idea
'cause it'll interrupt

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.510
the stages of your sleep
and you're gonna wake up

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.530
feeling not refreshed.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.600
So my sleepy time tea is chamomile,

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.300
no caffeine, it's an herbal tea.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.500
There's something relaxing about it.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.820
I love it, it's my go-to drink.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.460
Next, the real secret of everything

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:40.810
comes from this yellow pad.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:41.643
(crowd oohing)

00:04:41.643 --> 00:04:43.210
It's nothing to do with this yellow pad.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:44.850
It's just where I'm gonna write down

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.180
the next things I'm about to tell you.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.250
First, create a to-do list
for the following day.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.190
'Cause the last thing you wanna do

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:51.437
is get into bed and start thinking,

00:04:51.437 --> 00:04:53.780
"Oh, I have to remember to
do this and this and this."

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:54.680
That's gonna keep you up.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.770
Get it down on paper,
get it out of your mind.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.830
Second, I'm gonna tell you
about the three good things.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.000
The three good things is something

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.390
I wanted to tell you for the longest time.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.390
I wanted to make a video dedicated to it

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:07.350
'cause I'm so excited about
the three good things.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.160
It's actually a process
meant to decrease anxiety

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.850
and depressive symptoms
for up to six months.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.220
And all it takes is every
night before you go to sleep,

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.040
to grab a yellow pad or a
non-yellow pad or your phone

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.480
and to write about three good things

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.950
that have happened to you that day

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.700
as well as a brief reason as to why.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.090
For example, for me, number one,

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.420
I'll say Bear listened to me.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.710
And the reason would be, lots of training.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.100
It could be something as small as that.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.690
It could be something as
big as your family member

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.680
had a graduation, you've met someone,

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.450
someone held the door for
you in the supermarket,

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:39.470
or you ate a yummy meal.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.020
It doesn't matter as
long as it's positive.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.100
The reason we're doing
this is we're retraining

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.130
our minds to let go of the anxiety

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.490
and think about the good things.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.470
Our minds naturally have a
baseline level of anxiety.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.280
That's how our ancestors survived.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.050
But now it's much safer.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.570
We're living in homes where
there are no natural predators.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.460
So we need to think about
the positive things,

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.360
less so about the anxious things.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.220
The patients that I've recommended this to

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.030
say it's a true game changer,

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.080
I stand behind it, they stand behind it,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.680
and it's been researched in
psychiatric medical journals

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.060
saying that it truly does work.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:09.923
Come on, bedtime.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.500
It's bedtime for me,
but I'm gonna leave you

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.030
with three things that I recommend

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.530
you do not do before going to bed.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:19.250
First thing, cell phone.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.930
Get the cell phones away from you.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.950
If you need to scan for urgent emails

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.920
from work or messages, that's fine.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.710
But don't go on social media,

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.650
check out what your ex's doing,

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.580
get FOMO, that's gonna
peak your anxiety levels,

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:32.550
definitely don't do that.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.230
Second, avoid heavy conversation.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.807
You get into bed with your
partner and you're like,

00:06:37.807 --> 00:06:40.100
"Why didn't you like what I was wearing?"

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.270
You're gonna be worn out.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:42.670
You're gonna have a low will power.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.810
You're not gonna give
the proper amount of time

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.010
to discuss the matter, so
save it for the next day.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.850
And for number three, no news in bed.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:50.790
There's so much anxiety

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.520
that's gonna be passed
on to you from there,

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.190
it's gonna make it
impossible to fall asleep,

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:55.970
save that for the morning
when you have your coffee,

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.920
your tea, you're ready
to go and face the day.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:06:59.730
And I can go to the bed without saying

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.130
good night to the newf.

00:07:02.037 --> 00:07:03.644
Good night, ol' puppy,
good night, good night,

00:07:03.644 --> 00:07:05.694
good night, I love you, good night, yeah.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.340
Leave your questions down below

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.703
and as always, stay happy
and healthy, and sleepy.

00:07:11.703 --> 00:07:12.840
(hands clapping)

